From 88aebda0dcffe8cbadf19cd918791cac15c7279c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: JM Jacinto <jjacinto@free.fr>
Date: Wed, 21 Oct 2026 12:22:49 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] ALSA: usb-audio: Add always-set-rate quirk for Vestax VCI-380

The Vestax VCI-380 DJ controller (UAC2, single internal clock, 48 kHz
only) reports 48000 Hz on its clock source right after power on, but
only configures its clock when it receives a SET_CUR sampling frequency
request. Since the reported rate already matches, the driver never sends
it, and the capture stream is corrupted: a block of samples is dropped
or repeated every ~90 frames (about 140 discontinuities per second,
heard as choppy sound). Playback is not affected.

The Windows driver always sends the request, which is why the inputs
work after the controller has been used on Windows without being
switched off. Sending a single SET_CUR of 48000 Hz to the clock source
(ID 41) from Linux fixes the capture stream until the next power cycle.

Apply QUIRK_FLAG_ALWAYS_SET_RATE to the device.
---
Tested by sending the same SET_CUR (48000 Hz, clock source 41) from user
space with libusb on a kernel without QUIRK_FLAG_ALWAYS_SET_RATE: the
recorded input went from about 140 discontinuities per second to none,
until the controller is switched off. The quirk itself could not be tested
on this kernel.

 sound/usb/quirks.c | 2 ++
 1 file changed, 2 insertions(+)

diff --git a/sound/usb/quirks.c b/sound/usb/quirks.c
index a54fabc..a7e5c38 100644
--- a/sound/usb/quirks.c
+++ b/sound/usb/quirks.c
@@ -2464,6 +2464,8 @@ static const struct usb_audio_quirk_flags_table quirk_flags_table[] = {
 		   QUIRK_FLAG_MIXER_PLAYBACK_MIN_MUTE),
 	DEVICE_FLG(0x19f7, 0x0035, /* RODE NT-USB+ */
 		   QUIRK_FLAG_GET_SAMPLE_RATE),
+	DEVICE_FLG(0x1aa1, 0x0240, /* Vestax VCI-380 */
+		   QUIRK_FLAG_ALWAYS_SET_RATE),
 	DEVICE_FLG(0x1bcf, 0x2281, /* HD Webcam */
 		   QUIRK_FLAG_GET_SAMPLE_RATE | QUIRK_FLAG_MIC_RES_16),
 	DEVICE_FLG(0x1bcf, 0x2283, /* NexiGo N930AF FHD Webcam */
-- 
